Bruno Kazer wrote:
> "stth" <inva...@invalid.invalid> wrote:
>
>> Barbarians also allow for a similiar "no clever thinking required"
>> approach. They even have the advantage of poison resistance.
>
> Not true. Since the Barbarians are so good at combat, they can "dive"
> right away.  I usually go down to level 4 or level 6 even as soon as I
> find the stairs, and it is here that a solid tactical skill is
> useful.  It is fun to get in almost over my head and use smart
> fighting techniques to dispatch everything that comes at me.

Melee combat tends to be a trap.  Until you've learned other combat
systems it's hard to ascend with melee because the crowds continue to
grow.  it gets progressively more tempting to fight a crowd.

>> Getting Excalibur is no minor thing though, as it is one of the best
>> weapons you can get, and getting it that early helps a lot. Valks also
>> start with a +3 small shield. Law Val also have peaceful Mines, unlike
>> Barbs who always have hostile Mines.
>
> Peaceful mines is not necessarily a boon.  The barbarian can easily be
> level 7 or level 8 by the time he reaches minetown.  Those gnomes are
> not particularly difficult. And I do not really like gnomes anyway.

With hostile mines I practice a cycle.  Go down and fight some.  If
possible eat some.  Then run back up the stairs and wander until fully
healed.  Back down the stairs.  Keep cycling until the entire level is
cleared and explored.  Then cycle at the next level.  There's no need to
count kills.

With peaceful mines I still want the objects of the peaceful monsters so
I count kills.  Four hostiles per peaceful.  If I fully explore and
clear a level of hostiles and it still has peacefuls I tend or not based
on health and other criteria.  Usually down to the next level I go. 
With peaceful mines the density of hostiles is lower so there is more
chance to eat corpses and stay well fed.  Eat at the first sign of
hunger.

Having learned that difference in strategy it stopped being an advantage
or disadvantage.  Just a call for different strategies.


